From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rustcc-build <rustcc@localhost>
Date: Wed, 3 Jun 2026 06:33:35 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] P09.x (1.13.7): subclass an imported C++ polymorphic base
 from Rust
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Lets a Rust `class D : CppBase` inherit from an *imported* C++
polymorphic class (cxx_importer-generated) with working cross-boundary
virtual dispatch: C++ calling a virtual through a `CppBase*` that points
at a `D` lands in the Rust `override`.

New attribute `#[rustc_cxx_imported_vtable = "<spec>"]` on the imported
base's `#[repr(C)]` struct carries its primary-vtable function-pointer
slots in C++ order (`zti=…;slot=<name>,<symbol>;…`). The chain-walk
(`virtuals_on_chain`) seeds the derived vtable from those slots; a Rust
`override` matched by name replaces a slot, a non-overridden slot points
directly at the real C++ symbol (or `__cxa_pure_virtual` for an
unoverridden pure virtual). `is_polymorphic_cpp_class` (itanium,
check_attr, cxx_bridge copies) treats the attribute as polymorphic so
the derived shares the base vptr at offset 0; `emit_typeinfo` chains the
derived `_ZTI` to the base's external `_ZTI`. Imported bases never emit
their own `_ZTV`/`_ZTI`/`_ZTS` (C++ owns them). `rustc_ty_to_cxx` maps an
imported base by name so it can appear in C++-mangled signatures (e.g.
the auto-generated `Deref for D`).

Supports concrete and pure-virtual base methods (single inheritance,
Itanium). Virtual destructors land in a follow-up commit.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
